South Korea warns North Korea to cancel spy satellite launch
SEOUL, Nov. 20 (Xinhua) - South Korea on Monday warned North Korea to immediately cancel its reconnaissance satellite launch program with a commitment to take the necessary measures if the project goes ahead. This is according to a release by the AFP news agency.
North Korea is said by South Korea to be preparing to launch a third reconnaissance satellite after two previous failed launches. Earlier in November, South Korea's intelligence agency said North Korea was in the final stages of preparing for a third satellite launch, as South Korean Defense Minister Shin Won-sik concluded Sunday said the launch could take place this week.
In a warning message to North Korea, Kang Ho-pil, director of operations of the South Korean Joint Chiefs of Staff, said: "We warn North Korea to immediately cancel ... the current preparations for Launch a military intelligence satellite. "But if North Korea continues to run the project, regardless of our warnings, our military will take the necessary steps to ensure the safety and security of its people."
