Fisheries officials: Motor-powered oyster fishing has a serious impact on riverine ecosystems and biodiversity
(Kampong Chhnang): All kinds of oysters are currently being transported by large boat traders at some places along the river in Kampong Chhnang province, all of which are transported from the river area of Kampong Thom and Pursat provinces, where traders hauled glue here and transported it by boat to Kampong Chhnang province.
Although such large-scale fishing and transport of clams is prohibited by the Fisheries Law, fishing and transport are still observed by some traders. Vehicles at some locations along the riverfront of Kampong Chhnang province were then loaded onto trucks parked on the national road for further transport.
He further stated that the oysters brought to Kampong Chhnang province must pass the inspection of his professional officers by carefully inspecting what kind of oysters are the oysters for the local market. He was also considerate and allowed to transport by car or bulldozer, but not more than 30 bags per car.
For large vehicles, it is strictly forbidden to transport them, if any, he will fine the transaction twice for non-recidivism and build a case to be sent to court. On the other hand, if all the clams are found to have a variety of chakras, also known as duck feed clams, he will not allow them to be transported back into the river, because these clams can not be eaten. That is, they can be transported across the border to sell to neighboring countries for various animal feed.
Not only that, the bottom of the Tonle Sap is also an important place for all of the above fish to spawn in the sediments of those rivers. The glue used by the machine-powered machines of the traders, which they have been operating as they are today, has been severely affected, including the eggs and juveniles at the bottom of the river.
That is why there is a law prohibiting fishing with machine power as stated in the Fisheries Law. Therefore, if our authorities can not prevent the use of motor fishing like today according to the law on fisheries, the good fish species as mentioned above will be extinct from the surface of the Tonle Sap Lake one day for sure. Koh Kong provincial authorities are seeking the clearing of state forest land in Dong Peng commune, Sre Ambel district.
(Koh Kong): Koh Kong provincial authorities are searching for those who cleared state forest land in Bak Angrot village, Dong Peng commune, Sre Ambel district, after the news that there were crooks, rich people, stealing and encroaching many hectares of forest land. State property comes as personal property.
Mr. Sok Sothy, Deputy Governor of Koh Kong Province, said on the afternoon of November 18, 2023 that he was assigning professional officers and Sre Ambel district authorities as well as environmental officers to search for individuals who dared to use forest clearing machinery controlled by Koh Kong Provincial Administration is located at Kampong Khlum, Peam Treng and Kong Cha points, Bak Angrot village, Sre Ambel district, Koh Kong province.
The Deputy Governor continued that the location of the land, which is therefore in the next geography, has already been cleared through Sub-Decree No. 30. However, the authorities are examining in detail whether the land has a title deed or not, or is enjoying agricultural benefits.
Mr. Sok Sothy confirmed that, however, if the land is still forest land and there is no agricultural cultivation, the authorities will take legal action according to the procedure. It should be noted that for the past two days, there have been many reports that many hectares of forest land owned by the state in Bak Angrot village, Dong Peng commune, Sre Ambel district have been cleared by criminals.
Hong Pros, deputy governor of Sre Ambel district, said that after receiving the information, he cooperated with district officials to inspect the actual site yesterday, but found no machinery or people were clearing the area. The suspects evacuated the vehicle from the scene and disappeared.
The deputy district governor said that the authorities have not yet cleared the information that Bun Loun is the perpetrator, while investigating in detail while finding more than 1 hectare of land that was encroached upon.
Regarding the forest land issue, Fresh News asked for an explanation from Mr. Vet Sonim, Deputy Director of the Koh Kong Provincial Department of Environment in charge of the South Cardamom Mountain Natural Resource Conservation Area, to find out how to take legal action against those who dare to encroach on the forest land. State, but his phone could not be reached.
