China has called the meeting between Chinese and US leaders in California a "warm" meeting. This is the first meeting after a verbal dispute between China and the United States over the Taiwan issue and later on a dispute over the use of Chinese air balloons, which the United States considers espionage, while China has said it was a balloon. Tracked the weather system but flew in the wrong direction.
A statement from the Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs said that US-China relations are "the most important bilateral relations in the world". "China's momentum of growth and stability is good for the United States and the world," the statement said.
But behind the warm words exchanged, there are still some tensions. One of the points is the reality of the economic struggle, with the United States restricting China's exports of high-tech technology. Laws affecting the export of chipmaking technology have been put in place to curb China's ability to develop state-of-the-art electronics, which went into effect this Thursday.
At Wednesday's meeting, Xi Jinping said such US measures "seriously undermined China's legitimate interests." Behind the warm exchange of words, there has been little progress on the biggest tension between China and the United States, namely China-Taiwan.
Experts in China and some US experts are concerned that the US statement in support of Taiwan will make the connection between the two countries unpredictable, especially the United States. Adherence to the one-China policy, but the practice shows the continued interference in China's internal affairs.
Last year, China's anger at the United States peaked when fears of war erupted over a visit by former US House of Representatives Speaker Nancy Pelosi. That Taiwan. That anger has led to the severance of relations between China and the United States, such as military ties and anti-drug control.
On Wednesday, when the two leaders first met, there were some signs of relief that the two leaders had agreed to resume military talks and reached an agreement on fentanyl. In the days leading up to the meeting, the United States and China issued a new joint statement on climate change.
