International Analysis: How does Israel hunt down Hamas tunnels?
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - While waging war against Hamas in the heart of Gaza for more than six weeks, the Israeli army is also trying to mitigate the risk of a major breakthrough. Hamas' vast and mysterious network of tunnels. To avoid the deaths of Israeli soldiers, Israel has used technology to find and destroy tunnels, including Hamas leaders.
From above, Israeli drones were responsible for finding the tunnels, while fighter jets led the attack using deep-ravaged bombs. On the ground, bulldozers were clearing the area of suspected tunnels, and unmanned dogs and robots were used to search the tunnels.
The Israeli military has been in contact with a number of technology companies to help with its war, including the participation of emerging technology companies such as Asterra, an Israeli company specializing in satellite submarine exploration technology.
