The following is the decision of Samdech Hun Manet: The European Union and the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations support the improvement of the University's fisheries curriculum.
Support for the continuous improvement of the Bachelor of Science in Fisheries curriculum with four universities to improve fisheries education in Cambodia. The four universities are: Royal University of Agriculture, National Institute of Agriculture, Prek Leap, National Institute of Agriculture, Kampong Cham and Kratie University.
In addition to strengthening institutional capacity for fisheries education, the program will also ensure that university students acquire the knowledge, skills and abilities that best suit the current needs of the job market.
The workshop is part of a broader effort aimed at improving the country's fisheries management by creating professional skills for young people who can address fisheries challenges. These workshops are taking place in Siem Reap from 14 to 16 November 2023 and from 5 to 7 December 2023.
Eric Baran, International Fisheries Research Specialist of FAO Cambodia, said: “We are responding to the current needs of the Faculty of Fisheries Science to help review and update the fisheries curriculum by incorporating current knowledge and technology. "Adapt to the growing local, regional and international labor market in the fisheries sector."
Mr. Hem Virak, Director of the National Institute of Agriculture, Kampong Cham, welcomed this special support for the improvement and development of the curriculum through the coordination of consultations with professors from various universities.
Also, the Vice Dean of the Faculty of Forestry, Fisheries and Environment of the Prek Leap National Institute of Agriculture welcomed the support. "This is the first time that the fisheries curriculum has been coordinated between the four universities," said Phen Sovathdy.
During the workshop, deans, professors, stakeholders and key players made detailed assessments of the expected outcomes of the lessons for fisheries to make them more relevant to the needs of the sector and the needs of the industry. University professors and staff also have the opportunity to provide feedback, improve and share practical experiences on existing curricula and lessons.
The first workshop was held in Siem Reap with the participation of 30 representatives from four government institutions and four universities. In total, the workshop was attended by 60 stakeholders.
Following these consultations, the curriculum and related lessons will be revised and finalized before being submitted to the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports for approval and becoming the standard for the coming year. Outstanding National Elite Team Disseminates AIDS to BELTEI International University Students
