(London): The UK economy stagnated in the third quarter of this year due to rising inflation and rising interest rates. According to the AFP news agency.
The gross domestic product (GDP) showed zero percent growth between July and September 2023, AFP reported, citing official data released by the National Statistics Office (ONS) on Friday. The rate was better than market expectations of a 0.2 percent decline, but lower than the 0.2 percent growth the country achieved in the second quarter.
The data comes a week after the Bank of England kept interest rates at 5.25 per cent, the highest in 15 years, to deal with high inflation and forecast slower growth next year.
"Economic activity continues to decline as high interest rates are putting a strain on consumer sentiment and people's savings," she said. "As real income (income minus inflation) starts to rise, as inflation slows, this is being offset by higher mortgage rates, which have been driven by a slowdown in inflation," she added. "Spending on housing."
