On the occasion of celebrating the 14th National Day for the Promotion of Rural Sanitation, Prime Minister Hun Manet would like to suggest the following important recommendations and initiatives:
First, the national and sub-national rural water supply and sanitation technical working groups must continue to pay attention to good coordination and cooperation with development partners, NGOs and all stakeholders to improve the rural sanitation situation. Even better.
Second, the Ministry of Rural Development and relevant ministries and institutions must continue to participate more actively and in all forms to accelerate the rate of rural sanitation coverage to achieve and achieve the goals of the political program of the Royal Government of the 7th legislature. Of the National Assembly for the construction and defense of the motherland, 2023-2028.
Third, the Ministry of Rural Development must continue to seek support and mobilize more resources from development partners as well as the private sector to participate in rural sanitation.
Fourth, the Ministry of Rural Development shall establish a clear and reliable data management system for rural sanitation coverage to be used as a basis for monitoring and evaluating the progress of rural sanitation promotion work.
Fifth, continue to encourage and support provinces, municipalities, districts, Khans, communes, sangkats and villages with high sanitation to achieve provinces, municipalities, districts, Khans, communes, and villages that have completed open defecation.
Sixth, more attention should be paid to vulnerable groups, the poor, indigenous peoples, widows, the disabled and those living in vulnerable areas.
Seventh, continue to make efforts to promote and support in all forms to the provinces, municipalities, districts, Khans, communes and villages with low coverage to achieve better sanitation rates and achieve the completed provinces, municipalities, districts, Khans, communes and villages. Defecation in the open air.
Eighth: Continue to promote hand washing with soap in the community, schools and public institutions through education and the establishment of hand washing facilities.
Ninth: Increase attention should be paid to raising awareness and practice of hygiene during menstruation and adolescent girls by having educational mechanisms in communities and schools in order to change the attitude of menstrual hygiene management. For the better.
10th: Care must be taken to provide services and guidelines for proper waste management in a technical manner.
In the last 10 years or so, the growth rate of access to sanitation has grown remarkably fast, with an average growth rate of about 5% per year and is recognized as a leading country. To accelerate sanitation coverage and reduce open defecation among developing countries in the world.
Currently, the rural sanitation coverage rate in the Kingdom of Cambodia has increased to about 85%. In addition, six provinces in the Kingdom of Cambodia have achieved the completion of open defecation, including: Svay Rieng, Prey Veng, Kandal, Kampong Speu, Kampong Chhnang and Kep. At the same time, there are 7,397 villages, 654 communes and 59 districts that have completed open defecation.
This proud progress is due to the attention and active efforts of the Royal Government, the close cooperation of development partners, NGOs, the private sector and the active participation of all stakeholders. In particular, it reflects the people's level of awareness of the importance of sanitation practices.
