Health officials urge all relevant ministries, institutions and development partners to make non-communicable diseases a top priority as the disease causes up to 64% of deaths in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Kol Hero, Director of the Department of Health Protection of the Ministry of Health, stated that non-communicable diseases are currently a public health challenge that the Ministry of Health is paying attention to, with intervention measures to prevent, reduce and prevent in order to promote Health of the Cambodian people as well as the general population.
The four non-communicable diseases that the Ministry of Health is addressing among the population are cardiovascular disease / hypertension, cancer, diabetes and chronic lung disease, he said.
"These four diseases cause 64% of deaths in Cambodia, and this figure is projected to continue to rise if there are no preventive measures, such as not changing our lifestyle and attitudes," he said. Individuals. "Therefore, I urge all relevant ministries and institutions, development partners to consider non-communicable diseases as a top priority and work together to reduce the burden of non-communicable diseases that can be prevented."
He said on the occasion of World Diabetes Day on November 11, 2023 that the common risk factors that cause these four major non-communicable diseases are tobacco use, dangerous alcohol consumption. Uncomfortable diet and lack of anatomy.
He added that the prevention and control of non-communicable diseases can not be successfully implemented through the Ministry of Health alone, it requires the participation of all parties.
He added that the activities of the Korean Association for Health Promotion for almost two years and with the help of KOICA not only improved the awareness of non-communicable diseases in Prek Pnov, but also made Improve the living habits of the locals as well.
"We will continue to work hard and hope that this campaign will lead to a healthier life and provide opportunities for all participants to practice a healthier lifestyle," she said.
Dr. Sin Chung Yen also emphasized that in line with the five strategies of the Royal Government of Cambodia, the KOICA Office in Cambodia will strive to help all Cambodians live a healthier life by improving and strengthening public health care facilities. Ability to provide health care services and support the Health Equity Fund.
