In Asia, France focused on southern China and countries in Indochina, including Cambodia, Vietnam, Laos, and Thailand. In these lands, Catholic priests have long been secretly involved in the preaching work through the people of Jesus.
Returning to Cambodia now, France and Cambodia reached a joint treaty called the Cambodia-France Treaty of 11 August 1863. This treaty states:
* Provide mutual benefits. The French emperor promised to keep Cambodia in peace and order, protect it from outside aggression, and assist the Cambodians in collecting tariffs, facilitating maritime transport.
In return, Cambodia agreed to give France a concession of land along Chroy Changva and allow France to cut down trees in the forests of the Kingdom of Cambodia to build ships.
Although the Cambodian side lost to the French side, some Cambodians felt that the Cambodians could accept the treaty of August 11, 1863, because France had not yet taken power from the Khmer king.
In the first domination, the French did the cold method. Then, on June 17, 1884, France besieged the palace and threatened King Norodom, forcing him to sign a convention that stripped him of all his powers. Finally, France made Cambodia a source of agricultural supplies for France by integrating Cambodia into the French Indochina Federation.
Realizing that the armed struggle for independence caused the people to suffer so much, the King of Cambodia, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk, began a diplomatic struggle to demand the independence of Cambodia.
In 1949, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk demanded that France terminate the Guardianship Treaties signed in 1863 and 1884. In the same year, 1949, he signed the Treaty of Independence, which France legally recognized the independence of the Kingdom of Cambodia. From 1952 to 1953, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk went on a crusade to demand 100% independent treasure for the motherland.
On November 9, 1953, by his great deeds, the Kingdom of Cambodia gained full independence from the French Republic. Cambodians across the country have hailed him as "the great national hero, the father of national independence."
To commemorate the great achievement of the French independence from the French colony, the Independence Monument was built and inaugurated in 1958 during the reign of King Norodom Suramarit (1955-1960), the father of Samdech Norodom Sihanouk. The Independence Monument is also a symbol of Cambodia's independence from France on November 9, 1953.
Cambodia always celebrates this Independence Day with a candlelight vigil commemorating the patriots who sacrificed their lives for independence from the French colonies, and the festival is held annually on November 9 and ends on November 11 is the deadline. On November 9, the Royal Government of Cambodia allowed students, workers and government officials to take a break to celebrate.
Although King Norodom Sihanouk, a key figure in the struggle for independence from France, has been dead for four years, his success in bringing Freedom from France left his people with unforgettable memories of his great crusade.
