(Phnom Penh): Cambodia is planning to hold its annual Clean Energy Week, a major event aimed at promoting and celebrating the opportunities that clean energy brings to Cambodia.
The event will take place from 16 to 23 November, including programs such as: seminars, workshops, exhibitions and awareness raising programs to showcase the innovations, successes and environmental benefits of Clean energy. The program will also promote cooperation between stakeholders in the private sector, the public sector and public awareness.
In recent years, Cambodia has been boosting its clean energy opportunities with a strong commitment to providing security, reliability and cost-effectiveness of energy systems.
Highlights of Clean Energy Week 2023 include:
High-level workshops: This program includes digital workshops, face-to-face workshops and discussions on topics related to clean energy, such as industrial carbon reduction, the development of clean energy skills and energy diversity. Experts will share insights on the latest technology developments and policy initiatives.
2- Clean Energy Exhibition: An exhibition that will showcase new technologies, products and services in the field of clean energy. Participants will have the opportunity to explore modern renewable energy solutions.
3. Student and Community Involvement: This program will not only be a platform for clean energy professionals, but will also include activities to engage and increase awareness of clean energy among students and the community.
4- Clean Energy Run Tour and Program: Registered participants will have the option of participating in a clean energy project visit to gain a better understanding of Cambodia's efforts in the field of clean energy in conjunction with the program. Run for Cambodia's first clean energy.
"I am delighted that Australia is a major sponsor of this year's Clean Energy Week," said Justin Wyatt, Australian Ambassador to Cambodia. Australia supports Cambodia's energy transition goals. "Clean energy provides opportunities for economic development and can support Cambodia's energy security and progress towards carbon neutrality."
"Through the Cambodia-Australia Partnership for Sustainable Economic Development (CAPRED), Australia will support Cambodia in increasing its use of renewable energy and promoting the capacity building of its staff," he added. "Energy connects solar energy with people in remote areas and supports energy efficiency through energy provider market (ESCOs)."
"New Zealand is pleased to support Clean Energy Week this year," said Jonathan King, New Zealand's ambassador to Cambodia. "This event is an opportunity to share innovative knowledge and best practices in the field of clean energy from stakeholders who are working closely on Cambodia's future emissions reduction efforts."
"Cooperation on our shared desire to mitigate the effects of climate change is a key priority for New Zealand's support for Cambodia," he added. "We are proud to be able to support Cambodia on this goal, not only through this event, but also through our support for climate resilient agriculture and effective water resources management."
Alissar Chaker, Representative of the United Nations Development Program in Cambodia (UNDP), said the United Nations Development Program (UNDP) in Cambodia is committed to promoting energy sector change through a common agenda that focuses on changing policy, technology and financial policies. To have sustainable economic development in line with the priorities and ambitions of Cambodia.
This integrated energy agenda also contributes to Cambodia's National Commitments (NDCs) for reducing domestic emissions and adapting to climate change, as well as the Climate Promise Initiative. UNDP, the world's largest provider of support to countries to strengthen their national commitment under the Paris Agreement.
