(Phnom Penh): On the occasion of the 70th anniversary of Cambodia's Independence Day, the French Embassy will be exhibiting photographs of Cambodia taken from the air and via satellite during the last 70 years. These photos were prepared by the French Research Institute for Development (IRD).
Thirty aerial photographs taken from 1950 to the present will show the topography of Cambodia over the decades, confirming the country's development. The exhibition takes place from November 8 on the fence of the French Embassy in Cambodia.
Earth observations based on image analysis obtained from satellite imagery or drones allow them to describe and monitor natural resources, as well as the arrangement and variation of those resources. The IRD develops and uses digital tools and innovations to analyze images for the purpose of obtaining information on landscape change.
"KHEOBS is a leading laboratory in training engineers and researchers, as well as strengthening activities and projects that use innovative equipment to observe the earth in Cambodia and at the regional level," said Jacques Pellegrini, French Ambassador to Cambodia. "This project reflects the quality of our cooperation in the field of science, as previously there were not many institutions in Cambodia that had access to these technologies."
In addition to the images above, there are also images taken by drones that are very clear. New imaging analysis techniques are being developed to study topographic variations. These techniques are widely used in Cambodia to address challenges related to environmental observation in collaboration with research institutions.
