(Phnom Penh): Major General Prak Soth, Deputy Director General of the National Peacekeeping Center, on behalf of General Sem Sovanny, Director General of the National Peacekeeping Center, opened the 6th ASEAN and Regional Mine Action Management Training Course within the framework of the United Nations. Supported by the French Republic and cooperation from Australia.
The three-week course features military officers from France, Cambodia and Australia and will provide training to trainees from 10 countries: Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand, Nepal, Bangladesh, Vietnam, Laos, Mongolia, Sri Lanka and South Korea. This training will be an important part of strengthening the friendship and solidarity in peacekeeping operations even better.
As the host country, Major General Prak Soth expressed his pleasure to host this annual event, which demonstrates the recognition of the capabilities and experience of the Cambodian Armed Forces in demining skills.
He added that the 6th ASEAN and Regional Mine Action Management Training Course in Cambodia is now very important to share information and experiences with each other in line with the focus on The mine sector by Cambodia has been identified as an 18th priority in addition to the 17 United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).
Lt. Gen. Prak Soth believes that Cambodia will also draw on new lessons learned and practice from the in-depth discussions of this training course to further enhance its capabilities and efficiency in its respective countries and in peacekeeping operations. For the future.
Col. Cryprien Martin said that the lessons of the training will be in line with international standards, full of sharing and exchanging knowledge with each other, especially focusing on the knowledge and skills necessary for planning the management structure. And facilitate on-site operations. At the same time, it will focus on navigating during operations in the building, which is an important part of completing missions.
