He made the remarks after being questioned by members of the media, citing a report by a foreign organization stating that freedom of expression, representation of the people and freedom of the press in Cambodia had fallen to a lower level. Some local civil society organizations working on human rights and democracy have always supported such reports.
In response to this, Mr. Pen Bona explained as follows:
No country in the world allows the exercise of rights without responsibility. Even Facebook has a set of community standards. If we dare to violate our freedom, it will be blocked. In Cambodia, the most worrying thing is not the restrictions on freedoms, but the biggest problem is the misuse of freedoms as defined by law.
In this sense, legal action against those who abuse their freedom does not constitute a restriction on freedom of expression, but rather the implementation of the rule of law. The press process is determined by the current law on the press. More than 2,000 media outlets operate in accordance with Cambodian journalism ethics and law.
Speaking of people's representation, in addition to the election, which is the largest democratic process, Cambodians can also raise issues with the authorities or MPs to solve. In addition, they can make direct requests with leaders at all levels, up to the Prime Minister, through social media.
Therefore, the freedom and representation of the Cambodian people is very broad. "The issue that needs to be addressed now is the use of freedom, which is linked to responsibility, because these two things are inseparable."
In this regard, Mr. Pen Bona suggested that civil society organizations working on human rights and democracy should contribute to raising the awareness of the people, as well as some politicians and journalists about responsibility, not to mention From their rights and forget about respecting the rights of others and respecting the laws of that country.
He said that exercising freedom without responsibility is as dangerous as driving without brakes on public roads.
Therefore, the spokesperson of the Royal Government urges us to work together to build a society with human resources with knowledge, responsibility and respect for the law in accordance with the pentagonal strategy set by the Royal Government led by Samdech Thipadei Hun Manet.
