Understand the business: More than 100 fish farms resume commercial fish farming as prices and demand increase
(Phnom Penh): In the past, since the beginning of April, about 60% of fish farms, or more than 400 farms that are members of the Cambodian Fisheries Association, have suspended commercial fish farming after they suffered losses. After the harvest, due to the increase in fish feed, but the fish sold cheaper.
But now fish farming has started to pick up again as fish prices and demand for fish have increased, which can help them make a profit from this business.
He explained that commercial farming is about raising only one species of fish in a pond, and that good juveniles are selected and that there is a good selection of feed for the juveniles. Unlike family farming, farmers raise a variety of fish in natural ponds for family consumption and only a small amount left for sale on the market.
He said the shift to commercial farming would ensure that by the end of this year, the supply of fish in Cambodia would not face a shortage, with both farmed fish and natural fish, while farmers are currently feeding fish from this November. Will start harvesting and releasing gradually.
He hopes that there will be enough fish by the end of this year and the demand for fish will increase from November, because from November is the dry season, a time when Cambodians often organize various festivals and weddings.
He added, "We hope that from November onwards, some species of fish may increase due to the festival demand for fish." For weddings, they do not take the fish we raise, they take the squid or other fish, but some also start to use tilapia, which weigh more than 1 kg. Therefore, the demand for this type of fish may also increase.
The fish that most Cambodian aquatic farmers raise are red fish, trout, catfish, catfish, catfish, catfish, tilapia and so on.
"This year's prices are good because last year they raised a lot and there were not many fish eaters, but this year we saw a lot of fish eaters, as most of the breeders have given up farming since Kovid and there are very few left," he said. That he does not raise fish, so this year is expensive.
Sales of juvenile fish, meanwhile, are on the rise, with orders for trout and soft catfish. However, the price of young fish remains the same.
He also said that his farm is currently expanding more ponds to raise fish and breed fish for sale commercially.
He expects that after his farm expands more hatcheries, the price of the fry will be lower than it is now so that buyers can make a profit.
At the same time, he suggested that local consumers eat local farmed fish to help farmers, while local farmed fish are also of better quality than imported fish.
Mr. Lem Phor Sothavarith said that compared to April, the price of each fish has increased between 1,000 and 2,000 riel, which is a high price for farmers to benefit.
