Po Sen Chey District Authority Distributes 706 Property Ownership Certificates to Residents in Lvea Village, Sangkat Choam Chao II
(Phnom Penh): The authorities of Khan Por Senchey, Phnom Penh, have issued 706 plots of land to the people living in Lvea village, Sangkat Choam Chao II, Khan Por Sen Chey.
The handover ceremony was held on November 4, 2023 in the presence of Mr. Sak Chankanharith, Governor of Por Senchey District and Mr. Plong Sihak, Director of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Land Office of Por Senchey District, Deputy Governor. Khan with a large population.
The director of the Office of Land Management, Urban Planning, Construction and Land in Po Sen Chey district said that Phnom Penh was selected as a priority in the registration among all provinces in Cambodia according to the plan of the Royal Government through the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction is due to complete land registration nationwide by the end of 2023, but has been delayed due to Kovid 19 disease.
He said that with the good cooperation of local authorities and the participation of the people, the landowners have facilitated the team to demarcate, measure and collect plot data, which facilitates the process of implementation. Of the successful systematic land registration, a total of 706 plots were collected.
Mr. Plong Sihak added that the certificate of ownership of the property is useful for the people, such as: as proof of legal ownership, and this card must be protected by law and guaranteed by the state to reduce land disputes as a reference in case of dispute. Land can be used as collateral for loans and so on.
The district governor continued, "The registration of the land and the granting of ownership have provided two important benefits: the benefit of our state and our people." The benefit for our people is to ensure the warmth, security of the land and the full right to occupy the land. ...
Mr. Sak Chankanharith said that the title deed can also enable people to use it for daily necessities, including being able to guarantee to borrow money from the bank to do other business. The advantage for our state is that this land registration provides a great opportunity for land management and better organization and facilitation of appropriate land use planning.
Mr. Sak Chankanharith stated that better land management is the basis for the state to consider collecting revenue from the national budget and provide facilities to promote investment, promote the national economy and bring prosperity to our nation and people.
Mr. Sak Chankanharith advised people to know how to use the card and use it properly to avoid using the wrong card that leads to loss, for example, to use in sales or mortgage to use the money that is not profitable. Come back.
Letters from readers! Attitudes of Cambodians towards participating in reducing the use of plastic
(Phnom Penh): Nowadays, Cambodians are very concerned about health issues, while everyone's health depends on diet, water, air, breathing, personal hygiene to be healthy to live with energy.
In order to turn everyone into a healthy person living in a beautiful environment, everyone is now more aware of the work of garbage disposal to promote well-being and improve hygiene. Therefore, the dumping of plastic into waterways and places must stop if humanity wants to save the planet for future generations.
"Plastic kills biodiversity, it destroys the planet, it destroys everything." According to figures from the International Oceanic Organization, at least 12.7 million tons of all kinds of plastic are dumped into the world's oceans each year.
For Cambodia, through the campaign "Today I do not use plastic bags" "We work together to protect the Cambodian environment" initiated by Dr. Eang Sophalleth, Minister of Environment, starting from September 1, 2023, initially organized in Forty-four district schools in Ou Raing Ov district, Tbong Khmum province, for more than two months, more than 2 million teachers and students participated without plastic bags.
More than a month of more than a month of non-use of plastic bags by more than a month through the Ministry of Environment's "Today I do not use plastic bags" campaign. Because they do not use the plastic bag once or twice a week, more than 8 million plastic bags are not used in one week.
Through this campaign, Cambodians have explored different ways to adapt by turning to other materials instead of plastic. So, if the mindset of the 17 million Cambodians reduces or stops the use of plastic bags, it will make Cambodia beautiful, clean, and free of water pollution caused by plastic.
Reducing the amount of plastics will play a more important role in climate change, especially the impact on human and animal health due to plastics.
Cambodia is not the only country facing this plastic waste problem, but this plastic waste problem is a common concern of people all over the world, so we must work together to reduce the use of both plastics. All in all the different forms that are possible.
In fact, in Cambodia, the participation of all people, and especially people who love beauty, in fact, the Ministry of Environment has already achieved the success of this beauty one step, thanks to the participation of teachers and students. More than 2 million people on the topic "Today I do not use plastic bags."
Cleaning up plastic waste is a joint mission of people in ASEAN and around the world, because the environment around us is unlimited. In addition, nearly 40,000 tons (37,000 tons) of plastic waste has polluted the Mekong River, a major source of water for irrigating crops, agriculture and humans, as well as ecosystems.
Participating in cleaning plastic waste from every citizen will bring the beauty of Cambodia, which is an attractive beauty that attracts tourists to visit Cambodia as well. In addition to cleanliness, planting and reforestation for greenery will maintain carbon neutrality, which as part of the community's additional income will contribute to sustainable development.
In response to the strong growth in the use of plastic materials, especially plastic bags in the past, the Ministry of Environment, in collaboration with relevant ministries and institutions, has introduced measures such as: Increasing the special tax on imports of plastic products and materials from 7%. 10% and charge for plastic bags (400 Riel per bag) in supermarkets and shopping centers in order to reduce the use of plastic bags.
