APSARA Authority restores ancient paintings of Wat Reach Bo temple with Indian government funding
(Siem Reap): The Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts and the Embassy of India in Cambodia inaugurated the preservation and restoration of hundreds of ancient paintings in the temple of Wat Reachbo in Siem Reap on the morning of November 3, 2023.
The Minister of Culture and Fine Arts, Dr. Pheung Sakona, said that she, as well as the people of Cambodia, are very proud to be involved in the project to preserve and restore valuable and prestigious ancient paintings.
Ms. Pheung Sakona added that the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Government of India have signed a financing agreement to preserve the ancient paintings in the temple of Wat Reach Bo since November 12, 2022. She firmly believes that our commitment to protecting these meaningful and valuable paintings remains strong.
Since 2005, the experts of APSARA Authority have been actively involved in the conservation and maintenance of the buildings of Wat Reach Bo, and from 2014-2020, Wat Reach Bo was inscribed on the National Heritage List of Cambodia.
Inauguration of a school building under the auspices of Rotary Club Rock Camton Fitzgerald through Vision Cambodia in Puok District, Siem Reap Province
(Siem Reap): Inauguration Ceremony of a School Building in Thmor Koul Village, a two-room building made of galvanized stone, located in Thmor Koul Village, Trinhor Commune, Puok District, Siem Reap Province, with the support of Rotary Club Rock Camton Fitzgerald. Vision Cambodia was officially inaugurated on November 3, 2023.
Following the interest of Steve Richards, a representative of the Rotary Club, Camton Fitzgerald, Sut Chentha, Executive Director of Vision Cambodia, said the organization was actively contributing to education and food supply, as well as the construction of school buildings. Rural bases in target districts in Siem Reap as well as in some other provinces.
He said that seeing the shortage of students in Bing Kara Thmor Koul Primary School, which is an adjunct to the presidential school and not able to go to school far away and because the school building in the village lacks classrooms. And then the organization built this new school building to facilitate the study of students.
He hopes that this school building will provide students with a safe and comfortable environment. Therefore, please all students and parents to help maintain this new school building and use it for a long time.
Mr. Sin Chanthol stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia has paid great attention to continue to build and develop human resources in all areas with quality and good virtues in response to the requests of socio-economic development. On the other hand, the principle of education for all is to demand equal access to education services that provide equal opportunities for all Cambodian children and youth to receive basic education in the system as well as non-formal education.
Stong District Governor Instructs Commune Administration to Increase Attention to Vulnerable People
(Kampong Thom): Mr. Say Kim Lam, Governor of Stong District, Kampong Thom Province, instructed the commune administration to increase the attention of vulnerable people. Mr. Say Kim Lam introduced this on the occasion of visiting and distributing relief items to 9 poor families in Chek village, Kampong Chencheung commune, Stong district, Kampong Thom province on the afternoon of November 3. 2023.
He instructed the commune administration to collect data and rescue the people, as well as report to the district administration to be able to rescue the local people in trouble in time and not to leave any people or One of the victims died of starvation without the knowledge of the authorities.
Mr. Say Kim Lam also instructed the administration of Kampong Chen Cheung commune, as well as other communes to update the poor and vulnerable families accurately to respond to their living conditions, as well as to improve the family economy and respond to The policy of the Royal Government of the seventh mandate under the leadership of Samdech Moha Bovathibodi Hun Manet to reduce poverty and prevent other phenomena from the local people.
Minister of Environment: Local economic development is an essential livelihood for people living in protected areas
(Siem Reap): Dr. Eang Sophalleth, Minister of Environment, said that local economic development by providing options for planting crops and raising livestock is a necessary livelihood and participation in planting more trees locally is Develop the economy of people living in protected areas.
Dr. Eang Sophalleth made this remark during a meeting with residents and local authorities in the community of Prey Thom Anlong Thom Protected Area in the area of Preah Jayavarman-Norodom "Phnom Kulen" National Park in Siem Reap province. Friday afternoon, November 3, 2023.
The Minister emphasized the Ministry of Environment's incentives for product development, promotion of economic activities that contribute to the protection and conservation of natural resources, especially eco-tourism services in areas with natural potential and benefits from carbon sales to get a refund. Help strengthen the local economy as well.
At the same time, Dr. Eang Sophalleth encouraged the people in the protected area community to join the Ministry of Environment in planting trees, which the Ministry of Environment launched the October 10 campaign to provide 1 million seedlings for free. Every year from 2024 onwards, to increase greenery, increase forest cover in accordance with the policy of the Royal Government, which stipulates that forest cover must increase by 60% by 2050.
It should be noted that during the visit to the protected area community in Preah Jayavarman-Norodom "Phnom Kulen" National Park, the Minister and the delegation of the Ministry of Environment met and visited the exhibition of community products, including pig farming. Non-timber forest products, beekeeping, sustainable agricultural products and investment planning, ecotourism and related infrastructure, including clean water and sanitation in Phnom Kulen National Park.
The Minister of Inspection requested the inspectors to adhere to the code of professional ethics, independent, impartial and responsible.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Huot Hak, Minister of Inspection, requested the trainees who completed the 5th Vocational Training Course on Primary Inspection, 7 + 8, when completing the mission, to adhere to the professional ethics framework as an independent inspection officer. Impartial and responsible based on legal principles.
Mr. Huot Hak made this request at the closing ceremony of the 5th Basic Inspection Vocational Training Course, Section 7 + 8, organized by the National Inspection Institute at the Ministry of Inspection on November 3, 2023.
The Minister stressed the importance of the inspection sector in contributing to the promotion of effective governance, transparency and accountability, as well as contributing to the prevention, improvement and implementation of measures to combat corruption, abuse of power and inactivity. As well as contributing to the strengthening of law enforcement, increase the efficiency of public services and strengthen the cleanliness in public administration.
The Minister instructed all the trainees to apply the new knowledge and experience to work professionally to help the organization and strengthen the work efficiency to contribute to the promotion of good governance, which is At the heart of the government's pentagonal strategy.
The Minister requested all trainees during the mission to perform well, adhere to the professional ethics of the inspection officer, stand on the principle of independence, impartiality and responsibility, based on the principles of law and must abide by Samdech Techo's five approaches are "mirroring, bathing, scrubbing, treatment and surgery."
On the other hand, we must adhere to the approach of Samdech Hun Sen Manet on reform by focusing on 4 main conditions: 1- There must be a clear vision, 2- There must be high commitment, 3- There must be honest participation and Fourth, there must be a dedication to serve the public better, cleaner and more honest in order to achieve the vision of a high middle income country by 2030 and a developed country by 2050.
Minister Huot Hak also handed over certificates to 47 graduates on that occasion. Minister Chhay Rithy Sen Chairs a Meeting of the Human Resource Management Committee of the Ministry of Rural Development
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chhay Rithi Sen, Minister of Rural Development, chaired a meeting of the Human Resource Management Committee of the Ministry of Rural Development to ensure the effectiveness of the work of strengthening the institution and strengthening the management of human resources of the Ministry successfully.
The Minister said that the strengthening of institutions and human resource development of the Ministry of Rural Development is a contribution to the implementation of the first phase of the Royal Government's Pentagon strategy, which has set the first priority is the development of human capital, which is the foundation to build. Cambodia is growing towards achieving the vision of Cambodia as a high-income country by 2050.
On that occasion, the Minister provided an opportunity for the members of the meeting to comment on the work related to the work of strengthening the institution, the division of duties of both framework officers and contracted officials according to the clergy.
In order to ensure the effective work of strengthening the institution and strengthening the management of the Ministry's human resources, Mr. Chhay Rithi Sen recommended the following:
* Continue to implement the registration of officials and establish an evaluation system to provide incentives through various means, such as promotion, medals or other awards to civil servants at both the national and sub-national levels.
* Update the duties of the unit at both the national and sub-national levels in accordance with the priority programs of the Ministry and assign officers in accordance with the skills and work requirements in the unit.
* Update the CVs and skill levels of civil servants at both the national and sub-national levels.
