Cambodia's first fish ladder worth nearly $ 100,000 was officially inaugurated in Sleng village, Sranal commune, Kralanh district, Siem Reap province.
(Siem Reap): The first fish ladder worth nearly $ 100,000 to be built under research and funding from the Australian Center for International Agricultural Research (ACIAR) and the Australian Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) has been inaugurated. Officially located at Sleng Dam, Sleng Village, Kralanh District, Siem Reap Province.
The ceremony was presided over by Mr. Khun Savoeun, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries; There was also the participation of a large number of people.
The fish ladder benefits the environment and the people as it allows the fish to move freely and access food sources and spawning grounds while maintaining a supply of fish for local fisheries. The fish ladder has been studied to boost the local economy as well as improve the livelihoods of the people who depend on it.
According to Justin Wyatt, "The Sleng fish ladder is expected to increase the fishing income of 5,000 families and help conserve more than 100 species of local fish. "It is an important step towards a sustainable future for Cambodia's fisheries and fishing community."
"The innovations created by these research partnerships have brought many benefits to rural fish-dependent communities for their health and well-being, and I am pleased to see this technology being expanded," said Professor Umberger. More to benefit the community in Siem Reap.
Mr. Pin Prak, Deputy Governor of Siem Reap Province, said that on behalf of the Fisheries Technology Project Team in Southeast Asia (FishTech), I would like to thank the Royal Government of Australia through the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, relevant ministries and relevant authorities. Facilitate all kinds of procedures for the construction of fish ladders showing the Sleng Dam in Siem Reap province.
On behalf of the Siem Reap Provincial Administration, Mr. Pin Prak would like to support joint initiatives that promote the sustainable development of water resources for irrigation, agriculture and fisheries in the river basins, as well as the development of geographical slopes around the Tonle Sap Lake. In Cambodia, where both water for irrigation and fish resources are still plentiful for all.
The main success of the Sleng Fish Ladder Project is due to the close involvement of the community in all aspects of the design and construction process. For example, there are local meetings with stakeholders that provide the research team with appropriate information to select the best fish ladder arrangement for the site.
FishTech will be implementing a number of project presentations in Laos and Cambodia with a three-year program. This project is an important part that can inform the development in Cambodia and the Lower Mekong Subregion in the construction of fish roads (or fish ladders) to help restore the movement and support the development of sustainable irrigation and Resistant to local community environment.
After the construction is completed, an evaluation will be made about the arrangement of the fish ladder to ensure that it is truly beneficial to the local fish and the people in the community. Based on the results of this assessment, the FishTech project will be expanded across Cambodia and Laos.
