The three South American countries have condemned the Israeli attack on Gaza in retaliation for Hamas, which has killed more than 1,400 Israelis and taken 230 hostages in the deadly attack. A surprise on October 7.
At a press conference, Bolivian Deputy Foreign Minister Freddy Mamani said that Bolivia had decided to sever diplomatic ties with Israel due to the extremist attacks on Gaza. The three South American countries have called for a ceasefire, with Bolivia and Chile pushing for humanitarian access to the Gaza Strip and accusing Israel of violating international law.
Bolivia severed diplomatic ties with Israel in 2009 under a government led by former President Evo Morales, expressing opposition to Israel's actions in Gaza at the time. Until 2020, diplomatic relations were restored under a government led by former Bolivian Acting President Jeanine Anez.
