The biggest change is that only 14.2% of Australians are now satisfied with their country's political system, up from 23.4% more than 15 years ago.
Australia ranks fourth behind Vietnam, Cambodia and Taiwan in terms of satisfaction with its governance system.
People born abroad are more satisfied with democracy than people born in Australia. Education has been found to be the clearest indicator of people's satisfaction with the political system.
The report found that confidence in the democratic process was lowest among those who did not complete 12 years of education, with only 67.5% saying they were normal or highly satisfied.
Four out of five Australians with a university degree say they are satisfied, while confidence is slowly declining for postgraduates.
A contributing factor to this decline is the epidemic, which has had a significant impact on confidence, with research conducted by the Grattan Institute found that low-income people are disproportionately affected. During an epidemic.
The findings of this study were taken from the Australian National University (ANU) Asia Barometer Survey and ANUPoll, a series of three global surveys. Australian National High School every year.
