Speaking at a press conference on the China-ASEAN Education Fair this morning, Cao Chean, President of the Cambodia-China Association for Education Exchange, said that the exhibition was organized to promote cooperation and exchanges between the two countries. China and ASEAN, as well as Cambodia, are involved in education.
He said that in the last 20 years, China's economy has been the second largest in the world, and along with the economic growth, the education sector in China has also improved, but Cambodian students have not yet There is still a wide understanding of education in China.
He added that according to the Chinese Ministry of Education, 15 countries currently send students to study in China, including 12 countries in Asia, seven countries in the ASEAN region, with Thailand ranked first, followed by Indonesia. Malaysia and Laos. Cambodia has the lowest number of students in Cambodia, ranking 15th.
According to figures from the Chinese Embassy in Cambodia, about 500 Cambodian students study in China every year.
He added that this is why we organized this exhibition to promote the education sector in the country to Cambodian students to understand, because this exhibition has 500 scholarships for Cambodian students and more than 50 universities from China and More than 50 universities in Cambodia are also participating in the exhibition, which is expected to attract about 20,000 students in two days.
He hopes that through this exhibition, information opportunities will be provided to students in Cambodia who wish to study in China.
Mr. Hem Sinareth, Director of the Department of Education, Youth and Sports of Phnom Penh, fully supported the Cambodian Secondary School Education and Orientation Association and the Cambodian-Chinese Association for Educational Exchange, which have worked together to establish a Chinese Education Fair. ASEAN, which will be held in Phnom Penh at the end of 2023.
"Of course, the China-ASEAN Education Fair is very important in providing academic information of Chinese universities to students in Cambodia who wish to continue their studies in the country," he said. "In the future, we can also attract Chinese students to study in Cambodia."
Dr. Tong Vannak, President of the Cambodian Secondary Education and Orientation Association, said that this exhibition is very important for 12th grade students who are preparing for the Baccalaureate exam and are ready to continue their higher education at various universities in the country. Cambodia is also going to study in China.
"I understand that the organization of the exhibition at the end of this year will be an important source of information for high school students who have graduated and continue their studies at the university to make decisions and choose," he said. "Focus on the major he wants, whether he wants to study locally or study abroad."
